"Heat Engines and Refrigerators",
"Student worksheet · High school Physics and College Physics 2e",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Physics</em> (high school), §§{#} and {#}, and OpenStax <em>College Physics 2e</em>, §§{#}–{#}. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/physics and https://openstax.org/details/books/college-physics-2e.",
"<strong>Used here:</strong> the Rectangle cycle (under Cycle), and the Carnot cycle and Run it backward: refrigerator setups (under Setups).",
"The picture shows the cycle on a pressure against volume (PV) diagram, with the gas in its cylinder beside it. The shaded area inside the loop is the net work done in one trip round the cycle.",
"The <strong>Corners</strong> panel gives the pressure \\(P\\), volume \\(V\\) and temperature \\(T\\) of each corner. The <strong>Energy per leg</strong> panel gives, for each leg, the work \\(W\\) done by the gas, the heat \\(Q\\) into the gas and the change \\(\\Delta U\\) of its internal energy. The <strong>Efficiency</strong> panel (Coefficient of performance for a refrigerator) gives the heat taken in, the heat given off and the net work.",
"The Carnot cycle’s temperatures are set with the <strong>Hot temperature T<sub>h</sub></strong> and <strong>Cold temperature T<sub>c</sub></strong> sliders under Cycle, in {#} K steps. Click a slider and use the arrow keys to set it exactly.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> (under Readings) stores the cycle, the gas, its temperatures, the heat in, the heat out and the net work. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the readings to this page’s Data table; <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> An ideal gas, taken round the cycle slowly. For a monatomic gas, such as helium, the internal energy is \\(U = \\tfrac32 nRT\\), with \\(R\\) = {#} J/(mol·K).",
"Follow the energy round an engine cycle one leg at a time, find the net work as the area of the cycle on a PV diagram, and compare the efficiency of real cycles and refrigerators with the Carnot limit.",
"<strong>The first law of thermodynamics</strong> says that the heat \\(Q\\) put into a gas goes partly into its internal energy and partly into work \\(W\\) done by the gas as it pushes a piston out:",
"\\[Q = \\Delta U + W.\\]",
"\\(Q\\) is positive when heat goes into the gas, and \\(W\\) is positive when the gas expands. For a monatomic ideal gas, \\(\\Delta U = \\tfrac32 nR\\,\\Delta T\\), and since \\(PV = nRT\\), also \\(\\Delta U = \\tfrac32\\,\\Delta(PV)\\).",
"At <strong>constant pressure</strong> the work is \\(W = P\\,\\Delta V\\), the area under the line on the PV diagram.",
"At <strong>constant volume</strong> the piston does not move, so \\(W = 0\\) and \\(Q = \\Delta U\\).",
"<strong>A heat engine</strong> takes the gas round a closed cycle. Back at the start, the gas has its original temperature, so over the whole cycle \\(\\Delta U = 0\\) and the net work equals the net heat: \\(W_\\mathrm{net} = Q_\\mathrm{in} - Q_\\mathrm{out}\\). On the PV diagram, \\(W_\\mathrm{net}\\) is the area inside the loop. The <strong>efficiency</strong> is the share of the heat taken in that comes out as work:",
"\\[\\eta = \\frac{W_\\mathrm{net}}{Q_\\mathrm{in}}.\\]",
"<strong>The Carnot limit.</strong> No engine working between a hot temperature \\(T_h\\) and a cold temperature \\(T_c\\) (in kelvins) can do better than the Carnot engine:",
"\\[\\eta_\\mathrm{C} = 1 - \\frac{T_c}{T_h}.\\]",
"The only way to raise it is to make the hot side hotter or the cold side colder. Since some heat must always be given off at \\(T_c\\), no engine reaches {#} %.",
"<strong>A refrigerator</strong> runs a cycle backward: work \\(W\\) goes in, and heat \\(Q_c\\) is pulled out of the cold side. Its <strong>coefficient of performance</strong> is \\(\\mathrm{COP} = Q_c/W\\), and the best possible, for a Carnot refrigerator, is",
"\\[\\mathrm{COP_C} = \\frac{T_c}{T_h - T_c}.\\]",
"A gas at a constant {#} kPa expands from {#} L to {#} L. How much work does it do? ({#} L = {#} m³.)",
"In one leg, {#} J of heat goes into a gas and the gas does {#} J of work. Find \\(\\Delta U\\). Does the gas get hotter or colder?",
"Find the Carnot efficiency of an engine between {#} K and {#} K, and between {#} K and {#} K.",
"In item {#}, doubling the hot temperature from {#} K to {#} K doubles how hot the hot side is. Do you think it will double the efficiency, raise it by less than double, or raise it by more? Write your guess and the reason for it before you open the demo.",
"No hazards in the virtual lab. Real engines run hot, at high pressure, with moving parts: never open one while it runs.",
"The link at the top opens the Heat Engine Cycles demo. Leave <strong>Amount of gas n</strong> at {#} mol for the whole worksheet.",
"<strong>Part {#}: the first law, leg by leg.</strong> Under Gas press <strong>Monatomic</strong>, and under Cycle press <strong>Rectangle</strong>, with the direction at <strong>Engine</strong>. The rectangle has two legs at constant pressure and two at constant volume. Read the pressure, volume and temperature of each corner from the Corners panel into Table {#}. For each leg, work out \\(W\\), \\(\\Delta U\\) and \\(Q = \\Delta U + W\\) by hand from the rules in the Background, and only then compare with that leg’s row in the Energy per leg panel. Press <strong>Record reading</strong> under Readings, then <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> and <strong>Clear</strong>: the rectangle’s reading fills the Data table.",
"<strong>Part {#}: the Carnot engine.</strong> Press <strong>Carnot cycle</strong> under Setups. Keep Cold temperature T<sub>c</sub> at {#} K. Set Hot temperature T<sub>h</sub> to {#} K and press Record reading, then do the same at {#} K, {#} K, {#} K and {#} K. Watch the efficiency at {#} K and {#} K: these are the numbers to compare with your guess in Before the lab {#}. Press Export to worksheet, then Clear: these readings go to a second copy of the table, and its graph is the one for Analysis {#}.",
"<strong>Part {#}: a refrigerator.</strong> Press <strong>Run it backward: refrigerator</strong> under Setups (\\(T_h\\) = {#} K, \\(T_c\\) = {#} K) and press Record reading. Then set Cold temperature T<sub>c</sub> to {#} K and record, and to {#} K and record. Press Export to worksheet: these go to a third copy of the table.",
